Question 3: Row Explosion from Joins

Scenario

Two small customer-contact tables are available. Each table has one row per contact record. The tables share personal columns (id, first name, last name, email). Your goal is to reason about how many rows result when inner-joining the two tables on each of four columns: id, first_name, last_name, and email. Recall that in an inner join, only those rows are preserved where the feature or attribute matches in both tables. (One row will be created for every possible matching pair between the two tables.)

Table A columns (one row per record)

id: integer (unique in Table A and unique across both tables)

first_name: string (some values duplicated within Table A)

last_name: string (some values duplicated within Table A)

email: string (unique within Table A)

created_at: YYYY-MM-DD (date the record was created)

Table B columns (one row per record)

id: integer (unique in Table B and unique across both tables)

first_name: string (unique within Table B)

last_name: string (some values duplicated within Table B)

email: string (some values duplicated within Table B)

source: string (e.g., "web" or "phone")

Your Task

For each join column (id, first_name, last_name, email), explain qualitatively what will happen to the number of rows when you perform an inner join between Table A and Table B on that column. For each case:

State how many rows the join will create.

Explain why.

For the join(s) that are most likely to cause row explosion, describe one practical mitigation strategy.
